## THE QUESTION OF THIS ROUND (challenge it, reframe it, or answer it)

OPEN[UPPER-TO-FLOOR]. Every boundary-tree instrument that touches N
yields N <= (something in D) or D >= (something in N). Name ONE
mechanism that bounds the geometric degree N from BELOW by a function
of the degree D of a degree-minimal Jacobian pair (equivalently D
from above by N). Candidates the coordinator sees, none tried:
 (a) the Jacobian condition BELOW the last major disc D_1 (lane
     running) — does [f,g] = 1 force a minimum contact deficiency?
 (b) a GLOBAL invariant that is not a boundary count: Chevalley–Weil /
     Galois-closure representation data (the H_1 of the Galois closure
     as a rho(G)-module); the source-side étale structure of
     E = F^{-1}(A_F) (its components are étale over A_F minus the
     cusp; E is reduced); the monodromy of the pencil on H_1 of the
     generic member with all vanishing cycles at infinity;
 (c) an ARITHMETIC / characteristic-p mechanism: reduce a minimal
     Jacobian pair mod p and use the p-curvature / Tsuchimoto
     correspondence or the finite-field point count of the fibres to
     bound N in D (row 20 of APPROACHES, never run as a formalism);
 (d) a DYNAMICAL mechanism: the Hamiltonian flows X_P, X_Q on the
     fibres (the symplectic datum was a wash for the genus, but the
     flow's completeness/periods are a different invariant);
 (e) an ANALYTIC mechanism: growth / Nevanlinna-type or Bernstein-
     type inequalities for the polynomial submersion u∘F, where the
     degree D enters through growth and N through the covering;
 (f) a THEOREM THAT THE BOUNDARY ROUTE IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL: prove that
     no function of the boundary tree data bounds N below, and say
     what class of arguments that excludes — a negative result of this
     shape would redirect the campaign honestly.
