    88	## 2. Part (1): CONTACT-DEFICIENCY, re-derived, with its hypotheses made explicit
    89	
    90	### 2.1 The statement and the proof
    91	
    92	> **THEOREM CONTACT-DEFICIENCY (re-derivation).** Let `f, g in C[x,y]` be **monic
    93	> in `y`** with `deg f = deg_y f = m`, `deg g = deg_y g = n`, and let `F = (f,g)`
    94	> be dominant. Put `t = x^{-1}` and let `phi_1..phi_m`, `tau_1..tau_n` be the
    95	> roots of `f - c_1`, `g - c_2` in the Puiseux field `C<t>`. Then for generic
    96	> `(c_1,c_2) in C^2`,
    97	> ```text
    98	>     N  :=  deg F  =  deg_x Res_y(f - c_1, g - c_2)  =  - sum_{i,j} ord_t(tau_i - phi_j) .
    99	> ```
   100	
   101	*Proof.* Both are monic in `y`, so the resultant is the **bare product**
   102	`prod_{i,j}(phi_j - tau_i)` with no leading-coefficient factor — this is where
   103	monicity is load-bearing (negative control below) — and it lies in `C[x]`. Three
   104	genericity clauses turn `deg_x` into `N`: the fibre is reduced (automatic for a
   105	Keller pair, whose Jacobian is a unit; generic `c` otherwise); the `N` points have
   106	distinct `x`-coordinates (generic linear change inside the gauge); and `deg_x Res`
   107	does not drop, its coefficients being polynomials in `(c_1,c_2)` so that the
   108	degree is maximal on a Zariski-open set of `c`. Finally `deg_x = -ord_t` on
   109	`C[x]` and `ord_t` is additive on products. ∎
   110	
   111	**Slope split.** If `l(f) = alpha H^d`, `l(g) = beta H^e`, `H = L_1^u L_2^v` and a
   112	shear makes both slopes nonzero, the `tau`'s split `(ue,ve)` and the `phi`'s
   113	`(ud,vd)`; the `ue*vd + ve*ud = 2deuv` cross-class pairs each have `ord_t = -1`, so
   114	`N = 2deuv - sum_{same-slope} ord_t(tau_i - phi_j)`.
   115	
   116	*Scope note (emphasis correction to DC §5.1).* Same-slope orders are `> -1` for
   117	free but `>= 0` — what makes DC's budget form a statement about *positive* contact
   118	— only under **Moh Lemma 6.1** (`delta_{s-1} >= 0`, `moh.txt:2905-2912`) and
   119	**Prop 6.1** (`delta*_{s-1} >= 1`). Off that normalisation the same-slope sum is
   120	negative and `N` exceeds the cross-class count on **all seven** control rows
   121	(row 0: `N = 4 > 3`); five of the seven do not even have a common `H`, so `2deuv`
   122	is undefined there (CONTROL 5a).
   123	
   124	### 2.2 Controls (CONTROL 1 and CONTROL 2 in the deliverable)
