   156	## 3. THEOREM FRONTIER-N — the pairing in the form the tree can eat
   157	
   158	The pairing above is written on the roots of `f - c_1` and `g - c_2`; Moh's tree
   159	is the tree of the roots of `f` and `g`. The two are related exactly, and the
   160	relation is the whole mechanism.
   161	
   162	For a Puiseux series `sigma` and `delta in Q`, write `sigma_delta` for Moh's
   163	general `pi`-root of radius `delta` on `sigma`'s path (`moh.txt:361-374`), and
   164	recall Moh's Prop 1.2: the multiplicity of `sigma_delta` as a `pi`-root of `h` is
   165	the number of roots of `h` in the disc. Elementarily,
   166	
   167	```text
   168	    ord_t h(sigma_delta)  =  sum_{j} min( delta , ord_t(sigma - h-root_j) )  =:  lambda_h(delta) ,
   169	```
   170	a continuous, piecewise-linear, nondecreasing function of `delta` whose slope is
   171	the number of roots of `h` still in the disc; `lambda_g(-1) = -n` and
   172	`lambda_f(-1) = -m` in the gauge.
   173	
   174	> **THEOREM FRONTIER-N (proved here; every dominant `F` with both coordinates
   175	> monic in `y` of `y`-degree `=` degree).** For each root `rho_i` of `g` let
   176	> `delta^0_i` be the unique level with `lambda_g^{(i)}(delta^0_i) = 0`. Then for
   177	> generic `(c_1,c_2)`,
   178	> ```text
   179	>       N  =  sum_{i=1}^{n} max( 0 , - lambda_f^{(i)}(delta^0_i) ) .
   180	> ```
   181	
   182	*Proof.* Let `B` be the ball of radius `delta^0 := delta^0_i` around `rho_i`,
   183	with `a(B)` `g`-roots. On `B`, `g(sigma) = g_sigma(pi)t^0 + ...` with
   184	`deg_pi g_sigma = a(B)` (Prop 1.2), so `g_sigma(pi) = c_2` has `a(B)` roots, simple
   185	for generic `c_2`. Hence the `a(B)` roots of `g - c_2` inside `B` follow the
   186	`g`-tree down to `delta^0` and then separate, pairwise at `ord` exactly `delta^0`;
   187	this is a bijection onto the `g`-roots of `B`, and `delta^0` is common to them
   188	(`lambda_g` depends only on the ball above `delta^0`). So for the corresponding
   189	root `tau` of `g - c_2`, `ord_t(tau - phi^0_j) = min(delta^0, ord_t(rho_i - phi^0_j))`
   190	for every root `phi^0_j` of `f` (generic `c_2` keeps the separation coefficient off
   191	the finitely many bad values), i.e. `ord_t f(tau) = lambda_f^{(i)}(delta^0)`.
   192	Finally `ord_t(f(tau) - c_1) = min(0, ord_t f(tau))` for generic `c_1`, and
   193	`Res_y(f-c_1,g-c_2) = prod_tau (f(tau) - c_1)` because `f - c_1` is monic. ∎
   194	
   195	Two readings, both used below. **(i)** `N` is a sum over the `g`-tree alone: the
   196	`c`-shift is absorbed, the only new object being the *frontier* `{lambda_g = 0}`,
   197	Moh's "blur at `2^0 = 1`" (`moh.txt:2884-2896`). **(ii)** `N` is a **deficiency**:
   198	if the root counts are *proportional* (`b(delta)/a(delta) = m/n`) along `rho_i`'s
   199	path wherever `lambda_g < 0`, then `lambda_f = (m/n)lambda_g` there, so
   200	`lambda_f(delta^0_i) = 0` by continuity and `rho_i` contributes **nothing**. `N`
   201	measures exactly the failure of proportionality — by Moh's Def 3.1, the failure of
   202	the *distribution-detector* property.
   203	
   204	**Verified**: `N(frontier) = N(resultant)` on all seven control rows (CONTROL 1),
   205	including the deliberate near-proportional row 6, where `N = 18` because
   206	proportionality breaks at the last level — the mechanism §4.4 quantifies.
