   358	### 4.5 The residual free datum, named and sized
   359	
   360	> **What the pairing needs beyond the major tower, exactly.** For each bottom
   361	> major disc `D_1` (radius `delta_1`, `a_1 = eV_2` roots of `g`, `b_1 = dV_2` of
   362	> `f`): the joint ultrametric tree of those `a_1 + b_1` points **below**
   363	> `delta_1`. Its entire effect on `N` is one rational,
   364	> `N|_{D_1} = sum_{rho in D_1}(-lambda_f(delta^0_rho))^+ in [0, a_1(-lambda_f(delta_1))]`,
   365	> attaining the top when the `b_1` `f`-roots separate from every `g`-root at
   366	> `ord = delta_1` (combinatorially realisable) and the bottom when the counts stay
   367	> proportional to `lambda_g = 0`. Summed over the bottom discs: `N in [0,U]`, cut
   368	> only by the global `N >= 2`.
   369	>
   370	> **Does Moh §6 supply it? NO — and not because §6 is weak.** §6 supplies the
   371	> *minor* discs in the strongest possible form (contribution 0). What is missing
   372	> is the *major* bottom, structurally: at `r = 1` the minor criterion reads
   373	> `#roots <= n/(n+m) < 1`, so every subdisc of `D_1` is "major" and would extend
   374	> the tower — but there is no `M_0`. **`D_1` is where Moh's recursion ends and `N`
   375	> is born.** It is not a datum to be *enumerated*: it is a bounded rational whose
   376	> range the skeleton already computes.
