   388	## 5. Part (3): Moh's four `n <= 100` survivors
   427	## 6. Part (4): the filter, and why it inverts
   428	
   429	### 6.1 The direction theorem
   430	
   431	> **THE FILTER INVERTS (this lane's decision).** A Moh skeleton determines an
   432	> **upper** bound on `N` and no lower bound. Every ball of the tree contributes a
   433	> nonnegative term `Delta(B)a(B)b(B)` to the contact total
   434	> `N = mn - sum_{B != root} Delta(B)a(B)b(B)`, so unknown structure can only
   435	> *increase* contact and *decrease* `N`; the only lower bound available is the
   436	> global `N >= 2`. Hence **"N <= 16" is satisfied by every skeleton with `U <= 16`
   437	> and is never contradicted: it rejects nothing.** The operative test is the
   438	> reverse, `U(skeleton) >= N_min`.
   439	
   440	This is the exact sense in which the charge's instrument is finite and decidable
   441	but does not point at the ceiling: it is a ceiling **on `N`**, and the campaign
   442	needs a ceiling **on `D` at fixed `N`**, i.e. a *floor* on `N`.
   443	
