   257	## 3. The intrinsic plane Keller line sweep
   258	
   259	The construction that applies to an arbitrary polynomial Keller map does not
   260	start with a rational inverse. It starts with target lines.
   261	
   262	### 3.1 Every target line gives an unramified affine curve map
   263	
   264	Fix a determinant-one target change
   265	
   266	\[
   267	 H=\alpha P+\beta Q,qquad T=\gamma P+\delta Q,qquad
   268	 \alpha\delta-\beta\gamma=1.                               \tag{3.1}
   269	\]
   270	
   271	Then \(J(H,T)=1\). For every \(c\), the affine curve
   272	
   273	\[
   274	 C_c=\{H=c\}
   275	\]
   276	
   277	is smooth: \(dH\) cannot vanish because \(dP,dQ\) are a basis everywhere.
   278	The tangent vector
   279	
   280	\[
   281	 \tau=-H_y\partial_x+H_x\partial_y
   282	\]
   283	
   284	satisfies
   285	
   286	\[
   287	 dT(\tau)=H_xT_y-H_yT_x=1.                                \tag{3.2}
   288	\]
   289	
   290	Thus \(T:C_c\to\mathbf A^1\) has no affine critical point. For generic
   291	\(c\), let \(\bar C_c\) be the smooth projective normalization. The
   292	meromorphic extension
   293	
   294	\[
   295	 \bar T:\bar C_c\longrightarrow\mathbf P^1
   296	\]
   297	
   298	has degree \(\mu\).
   299	
   300	Separate the boundary punctures into
   301	
   302	\[
   303	 \Pi_\infty=\{p:\bar T(p)=\infty\},\qquad
   304	 \Pi_f=\{q:\bar T(q)\in\mathbf A^1\}.
   305	\]
   306	
   307	Write \(e_p=-\operatorname{ord}_pT\) at a pole and
   308	\(e_q=\operatorname{ord}_q(T-T(q))\) at a finite puncture. If
   309	\(r_\infty=|\Pi_\infty|\), then the exact pole/differential ledger is
   310	
   311	\[
   312	\begin{aligned}
   313	 &\sum_{p\in\Pi_\infty}e_p=\mu,\\
   314	 &\sum_{p\in\Pi_\infty}(e_p-1)=\mu-r_\infty,\\
   315	 &\sum_{q\in\Pi_f}(e_q-1)=2g(\bar C_c)-2+\mu+r_\infty,       \tag{3.3}\\
   316	 &\operatorname{Poles}(dT)=\sum_{p\in\Pi_\infty}(e_p+1)p,
   317	   \qquad \deg\operatorname{Poles}(dT)=\mu+r_\infty.
   318	\end{aligned}
   319	\]
   320	
   321	Equation (3.3) is just Riemann--Hurwitz plus (3.2). It is exact and useful
   322	for detecting a wrong pole book, but by itself it is an identity, not a
   323	kill.
   324	
   325	Chau proves that the universal plane Jacobian conjecture is equivalent to
   326	the assertion that \(Q\) has only one pole on \(P=0\). At the individual-map
   327	level, the one-pole hypothesis supplies the familiar one-place/degree
   328	divisibility route to invertibility. Thus a successful sweep which forces
   329	\(r_\infty=1\) for a Keller pair finishes the map; merely recovering
   330	\(\sum e_p=\mu\) does not.
   331	
   332	### 3.2 Sweep the tangent lines of the nonproperness curve
   333	
   334	Assume \(F\) is not proper and let \(A_i\) be a component of its
   335	nonproperness curve. On a smooth parametrized chart write
   336	
   337	\[
   338	 a(t)=(u(t),v(t)).
   339	\]
   340	
   341	Its tangent line is
   342	
   343	\[
   344	 L_t:\quad
   345	 H_t(U,V)=c_t,qquad
   346	 H_t=v'(t)U-u'(t)V,qquad
   347	 c_t=v'(t)u(t)-u'(t)v(t).                                 \tag{3.4}
   348	\]
   349	
   350	Choose a complementary \(T_t\) over \(\mathbf C(t)\) so that
   351	\(\det(H_t,T_t)=1\), and form the relative pullback
   352	
   353	\[
   354	 \mathcal C_i=
   355	 \{(x,y,t):H_t(P(x,y),Q(x,y))=c_t\}.                       \tag{3.5}
   356	\]
   357	
   358	On every affine fiber, (3.2)--(3.3) apply. This is the exact plane
   359	tangent-line family attached to \(F\).
   360	
   361	There is a concrete local calculation at a dicritical boundary component.
   362	After resolving \(F\), take analytic coordinates \((s,z)\) with boundary
   363	\(z=0\), the dicritical mapping along \(a(s)\), and generic transverse
   364	order \(m\):
   365	
   366	\[
   367	 \bar F(s,z)=a(s)+z^m b(s)+O(z^{m+1}).                     \tag{3.6}
   368	\]
   369	
   370	If \(L_t\) has contact \(\kappa=I_{a(t)}(A_i,L_t)\) and both leading
   371	coefficients are nonzero, then the pulled-back line has initial local
   372	equation
   373	
   374	\[
   375	 A(s-t)^\kappa+Bz^m+\text{higher terms}=0.                 \tag{3.7}
   376	\]
   377	
   378	The binomial Newton normalization of (3.7) has
   379	\(g_0=\gcd(m,\kappa)\) branches. On each branch,
   380	\(T_t-T_t(a(t))\) has ramification index \(m/g_0\), so the total local
   381	ramification contribution is
   382	
   383	\[
   384	 g_0\left(\frac m{g_0}-1\right)=m-g_0.                    \tag{3.8}
   385	\]
   386	
   387	For a transverse line \(\kappa=1\), this is \(m-1\). For an ordinary
   388	tangent \(\kappa=2\), an even \(m\) splits into two branches and lowers
   389	the contribution to \(m-2\); an odd \(m\) stays one branch with
   390	contribution \(m-1\). Equations (3.7)--(3.8) are the genuinely
   391	dimension-two tangent-sweep datum: contact of two curves becomes an exact
   392	gcd/parity constraint on escaping sheets.
   393	
   394	### 3.3 What proof success would mean
   395	
   396	Two possible successes have different strength.
   397	
   398	- **Full pole removal:** prove that the algebraic inverse-coordinate
   399	  functions on the normalization of (3.5) have no divisorial poles over
   400	  the tangent locus. If this can be done for every component of \(A(F)\),
   401	  sheets cannot escape, \(F\) is proper, and a proper étale endomorphism of
   402	  \(\mathbf A^2_\mathbf C\) is an automorphism. This would prove JC2.
   403	- **One-pole landing:** use (3.7)--(3.8), the boundary canonical divisor,
   404	  and the global family to force \(r_\infty=1\) for one complementary
   405	  coordinate. Chau's criterion then finishes that map. A theorem applying
   406	  this to every hypothetical counterexample would also prove JC2.
   407	
   408	No such theorem is presently available.
   409	
   410	**CONJECTURE TS-L (tangent-pole landing).** For a nonproper plane Keller
   411	map, the local tangent specializations (3.7)--(3.8), simultaneously over all
   412	dicritical components and with the global pole divisor of \(T_t\), are
   413	incompatible with (3.3) unless one target-linear pencil has one pole.
   414	
   415	This is the exact missing bridge, not a proved lemma. It may be false:
   416	Riemann--Hurwitz allows genus and other boundary components to absorb the
   417	one-unit parity changes. A credible proof must control the *relative*
   418	normalization (3.5), its genus jumps, and all pole sections; a single-fiber
   419	count cannot do so.
