   156	## 3. Keller specialization and the exact pole length
   157	
   158	Let `(f,g)` be a polynomial Keller pair.  Every affine fibre of `f` is smooth,
   159	because `df=0` would force `df wedge dg=0`.  On such a fibre the tangent
   160	vector
   161	
   162	```text
   163	T=(-f_y,f_x)
   164	```
   165	
   166	satisfies
   167	
   168	```text
   169	dg(T)=f_x*g_y-f_y*g_x=1.
   170	```
   171	
   172	Hence `g` restricted to the affine fibre is unramified.  For a generic fibre,
   173	all of the diagonal contact (2.1) is therefore at its boundary.
   174	
   175	Let the distinct pole ends have pole orders `e_1,...,e_s`.  They are exactly
   176	the points over infinity for `h`, so
   177	
   178	```text
   179	sum_j e_j=d.
   180	```
   181	
   182	Applying the local calculation with the target coordinate `1/g` gives
   183	
   184	```text
   185	length Ram_infinity(h)
   186	  =sum_j(e_j-1)
   187	  =d-s.                                                  (3.1)
   188	```
   189	
   190	This proves the candidate `d-s` statement at exact generic-fibre scope.
   191	Notice that it is true for any finite curve cover after all finite branch
   192	points have been deleted from its affine source.  The open pair square by
   193	itself therefore has not yet used polynomial `A2` origin.
   194	
   195	## 4. What the other diagonal contacts count
   196	
   197	Under the promoted delta-gate-passed quotient package, a generic `f`-fibre
   198	has `d_i` finite-value ends belonging to quotient line `i`, and the local
   199	degree of `g` at each of them is the high baseline `b_i`.  Consequently the
   200	finite-value part of (2.1) has length
   201	
   202	```text
   203	R_fin=sum_i d_i*(b_i-1),                                (4.1)
   204	```
   205	
   206	not `sum_i b_i`.  With `n=sum_i d_i`, (2.2), (3.1), and
   207	(4.1) give
   208	
   209	```text
   210	(d-s)+sum_i d_i*(b_i-1)=2G-2+2d,
   211	sum_i d_i*b_i=d+2G+s+n-2=d-chi_gen.                    (4.2)
   212	```
   213	
   214	Thus the fibre-square calculation independently recovers the promoted
   215	generic deficit/Riemann--Hurwitz identity.  It supplies no inequality.
   216	
   217	There are three distinct ranks here and they must not be conflated:
   218	
   219	```text
   220	pole diagonal contact:              d-s,
   221	finite-end diagonal contact:        sum_i d_i*(b_i-1),
   222	one-baseline-per-quotient-line:     sum_i b_i.
   223	```
   224	
   225	The quotient-collision event count
   226	`sum_i b_i*(d_i-1)` is different again: it records ramification of the
   227	parameter maps `P_i` as `f` varies, not ramification of `g` on one fixed
   228	generic fibre.  A surface-level pair square might relate these objects, but
   229	the generic-fibre residual divisor does not do so automatically.
