+## 5. Decisive desk experiment and strongest attacks
+
+I ran two read-only queries on the reviewed `box/moh_skeleton_N.py` enumerator; no repository file was written. The first counted branch-robust groups with `4 <= U_rob < 5` at the MOH-SHARP-2 degrees:
+
+```text
+D                         105   108   112   117   120
+old H2 groups pinned N=4   14    40     0     0     5
+```
+
+The second implemented `(SD6)` as a deliberately permissive rational knapsack. For each group it collected every enumerated lower-`V` branch type, assigned resource `V_2` and weight
+
+```text
+w(V) = e V_2 [-lambda_f(delta_1(V))],
+```
+
+allowed arbitrary repetitions and unused resource subject only to `sum V_2 <= u`, and asked whether the exact rational sum can equal 4. This drops orbit multiplicities, Moh (8)–(11), and mutual attachment constraints, so it over-approximates actual packets. A hit proves nothing; a no-hit is safe conditional on the branch-type exhaustiveness audit.
+
+```text
+D                         105   108   120   total
+old pin-4 groups            14    40     5      59
+permissive SD6 survivors     3    16     2      21
+rejected                     11    24     3      38
+```
+
+Wall time was 25.5 seconds after a sub-second extraction; memory stayed desk-scale. This is a qualitative change: the first lower-side instrument rejects 38 of the 59 first-window targets even after deliberately enlarging the branch-packet space. It also kills the visually tight D=105 example `M_2=-35`; its single-tower endpoint `360/83` must not be treated as attained, but no multiset of any allowed branch weights for that group sums to 4.
+
+The strongest proof attack is now: independently review `(SD1)`–`(SD6)`, add a fail-closed exact-weight mode, rerun all 418 targets, and then impose genuine orbit packets on the survivors. If the 21 permissive hits all disappear, the pinned-N=4 realization list closes without a coefficient Gröbner system. If some remain, their exact packets are the only rational D1 configurations the coefficient endgame needs to see.
+
+The strongest counterexample attack changes accordingly. Use the surviving D=105 group
+
+```text
+(D,m,K;d,e) = (105,42,21;2,5)
+M = (56,103),       d-chain = 105 > 21 > 7 > 1,
+V_s = 4,            (u,v) = (12,9),
+```
+
+with the permissive packet of four bottom-major discs having `V_2=3`. For each disc,
+
+```text
+delta_1=23/30,  lambda_g=-1/6,  lambda_f=-1/15,
+q=eV_2=15,      w=q(-lambda_f)=1;
+```
+
+four weights sum to `N=4` and consume `sum V_2=12=u`. This is cleaner than trying to force 4 below an unattained `360/83` endpoint. It is still only a permissive survivor: first require that four copies form complete Puiseux orbits and satisfy Moh (8)–(11). If they do, construct `g`, use `(SD1)` to integrate its branch values, and solve the global kappa/mate equation. If they do not, discard it without solver work and take the next exact packet.
+
+The branch-count output also answers the packet's concrete choice: start at D=105, not D=120. D=105 minimizes both coordinate degrees and has a depth-three chain. D=120 has fewer old pin groups but larger degrees and root packets. D=112 and 117 have no old pin-4 group at all.
