+
+The overstatement is: **the lane has not proved that the boundary route itself can never give a floor.** The interval
+
+```text
+0 <= N|D_1 <= a_1(-lambda_f(delta_1))
+```
+
+is the range allowed by Moh's skeleton and abstract ultrametric completion. Its lower endpoint is not attained by an actual Keller pair. The review says explicitly that the full Jacobian may constrain the subtree and that Moh does not (`xmodel/n-on-the-tree-review-grok46-20260902.md:137-151`). Integration #16 itself reserves exactly this exception (`xmodel/integration16-coordinator-fable51-20260902.md:109-115`). Thus “as built” is correct; “no boundary argument” is not licensed. Candidate (f) must be stated as a no-go for arguments factoring through Moh (1)–(13) plus the major skeleton, not for arguments using new Jacobian coefficients below `D_1`.
+
+There is also a plain factual error in candidate (c). APPROACHES row 20 was run as a standalone formalism lane on 2026-08-23. The canonical inverse-Jacobian connection has identically zero p-curvature, while Tsuchimoto's non-tautological object starts with a Weyl-algebra endomorphism and is not defined on an arbitrary commutative Keller map (`xmodel/sol-pcurvature.md:3-30,423-426,501-528`). That branch is `KNOWN-HOLLOW/retire`, not “never run.” Finite-field realization remains a different, one-way falsification tool.
+
+My allocation answer is consequently direct. Give `D_1` one bounded, exact pilot. If it does not expose a positive Jacobian deficiency at the first post-`delta_1` levels, stop treating a universal `D <= C(N)` as the primary proof target. Move the proof flagship to the Moh endgame—uniform elimination of finite-depth, unbounded-size skeletons—and run the exact same `D_1` machinery backwards on the counterexample side. Keep a small global-invariant probe, but do not fund five speculative ceiling languages at once. The depth count is finite; the coefficients and sizes are not. That is an elimination/realization problem, not evidence that another scalar ledger is waiting to be found.
+
+## 2. A new exact cross-connection: the `D_1` self-different
+
+The missing joint `f`/`g` subtree can be reduced to a one-colour subtree before any large computation. Work in Moh's monic gauge, put `t=x^{-1}`, take generic `c_2`, and let `tau_i` be the Puiseux roots of `g-c_2`. Set
+
+```text
+h_i(x) = f(x,tau_i(x)).
+```
+
+Implicit differentiation and `[f,g]=1` give the exact branch identity
+
+```text
+d h_i/dx = f_x - f_y g_x/g_y = 1/g_y(x,tau_i).                 (SD1)
+```
+
+If `h_i` has pole order `p_i=-ord_t h_i>0`, then differentiation changes the `t`-order from `-p_i` to `1-p_i`, hence
+
+```text
+p_i = 1 + ord_t g_y(x,tau_i).
+```
+
+If `h_i` is finite, its first nonconstant algebraic term has positive rational `t`-order, so `1+ord_t g_y<0`; equality zero would integrate to a logarithm and cannot occur in the Puiseux field. Because `g` is monic,
+
+```text
+g_y(x,tau_i) = product_{j != i}(tau_i-tau_j).
+```
+
+Combining this with CONTACT-DEFICIENCY yields the exact, review-needed formula
+
+```text
+N = sum_i max(0, 1 + ord_t g_y(x,tau_i))
+  = sum_i max(0, 1 + sum_{j != i} ord_t(tau_i-tau_j)).          (SD2)
+```
+
+The first consequence is already stronger than a change of coordinates on the same free datum. Let `B` be a bottom-major disc of radius `delta_B=delta_1`, and let `tau_i` be a shifted `g-c_2` root following a root `rho_i` in `B`. The shifted roots preserve every contact visible at radius `delta_B`, so
+
+```text
+ord_t g_y(tau_i)
+  = sum_{j != i} ord_t(tau_i-tau_j)
+ >= lambda_g(delta_B)-delta_B.                              (SD3)
+```
+
+The subtraction removes the self-root's clipped contribution `delta_B`. Equations `(SD2)` and `(SD3)` give a lower bound per root. RADIUS-ORDER and `M_1=-m`, with `m=Kd`, `n=Ke`, simplify it to
+
+```text
+p_i >= 1+lambda_g(delta_B)-delta_B
+     = d(1-delta_B)/(d+e) =: c_B > 0.                       (SD4)
+```
+
+But FRONTIER-N and monotonicity of `lambda_f` give the opposite inequality
+
+```text
+p_i <= -lambda_f(delta_B)
+     = d(1-delta_B)/(d+e) = c_B.                            (SD5)
+```
+
+Therefore equality is forced. This is the proposed **THEOREM D1-SELF-DIFFERENT** (elementary derivation here; not yet independently reviewed): every shifted root in a bottom-major disc contributes exactly `c_B`, while DETECTOR-NULL makes every maximal minor disc contribute zero. Consequently
+
+```text
+N = sum_{bottom-major B} q_B c_B,
+q_B = e V_2(B),
+c_B = d(1-delta_{1,B})/(d+e),
+sum_B q_B <= u e.                                           (SD6)
+```
+
+Different bottom branches may have different lower `V` data and different radii; no common `c_B` is assumed. Individual weights may be rational and integrality is required only after complete Puiseux/Galois orbit packets are summed. The safe computational relaxation is therefore a rational knapsack over **all** allowed branch types, permitting even arbitrary repetitions and unused major-root mass. A failure to make an integer `N` in this over-approximation is a kill; a hit is only a candidate until Moh's orbit restrictions (8)–(11) and actual root-tree attachment are imposed.
+
+This directly revises the opening verdict. Moh's major skeleton alone is upper-only, but the full Jacobian condition below `D_1` supplies a matching self-different floor term by term. The supposedly arbitrary finer contact cannot change the weight. The remaining datum is a finite branch-partition problem, not a free joint subtree. That is a concrete answer to `OPEN[UPPER-TO-FLOOR]`, though a cofinal `N >= phi(D)` still requires solving `(SD6)` uniformly.
+
+This also connects two lanes that the present ranking keeps separate. For a fixed candidate `g`, finding `f` is the linear Hamiltonian equation `[f,g]=1`; the banked kappa class vanishes exactly when a polynomial mate exists (`xmodel/hamiltonian-kappa-gate-20260824.md:49-80`). The kappa module has no universal receiver and remains a bad global ceiling strategy (`xmodel/hamiltonian-kappa-gate-20260824.md:180-198`), but on one typed `D_1` skeleton it is an exact obstruction/certificate engine. Candidate (d) should therefore be lowered as a global flow proposal and reused as the local solver inside candidate (a).
+
+The first cheap gate is now the exact `(SD6)` partition, followed by the no-log condition in `(SD1)`: every branchwise Laurent expansion of `dx/g_y` must have zero residue before it can be the differential of an algebraic `h_i`. Passing both is not a Keller witness; failing either is an exact rejection in its stated scope. This is the strongest proof attack in this submission.
